---
/*
 * `/data/workday-denominator` — the denominator behind Korea's monthly indicators.
 * (seoulmarkets.com/data/workday-denominator)
 *
 * ── Why this page (2026-10-12 19:0x · 5번) ───────────────────────────
 *   네 번째 데이터 지면이다. 오늘 고친 `check-쥔자료에-안쓴축이-있나.mjs` 가
 *   「지면도 기사도 «없는» 축 30개」를 내놓았고 그 가운데 골랐다.
 *   ⛔ 짓기 전에 `check-이축으로-이미낸게있나.mjs` 로 확인했다 — 걸리는 것이 없었다.
 *     (다만 그 자료는 열쇠가 한글이라 그 자가 얇게 봤다. 자가 그렇게 적는다.)
 *
 *   고른 까닭 — **영어권 손님이 한국 월별 지표를 견줄 때 반드시 알아야 하는데
 *   아무도 안 알려 주는 것**이다.
 *   ```
 *     2월 평균 18.5일  vs  7월 평균 21.2일   → 14.4% 차이
 *     2011년 21.2  →  2025년 19.1           → 15년에 9.9% 줄었다
 *     그리고 그 시리즈에 **이음매가 둘**
 *   ```
 *
 *   ⭐ 이 지면에만 있는 것 — **이음매의 크기를 계절성과 갈라 본다.**
 *     자료가 적은 변화율은 12월→1월 «한 달» 비교라 계절성이 섞여 있다(-4.5%).
 *     앞뒤 열두 달 평균으로 보면 -1.2% 다. **둘 다 적는다** —
 *     어느 쪽도 거짓이 아니고 무엇을 견주었나가 다를 뿐이다.
 *
 * ── ⛔ What this page must not become ────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ 「왜 줄었나」를 말하지 않는다. 주 52시간·대체공휴일·표본 변화 — 다 그럴듯하지만
 *   우리는 안 쟀다. 얼마나 움직였나까지다.
 * ⛔ 이음매를 이어 붙이지 않는다. 보정 계수를 만들면 그 순간 «우리 수»가 된다.
 * ⛔ 열두 달이 안 찬 해를 온전한 해와 나란히 놓지 않는다.
 * ⛔ English only — 사장님 2026-08-05 「우리 손님은 영어권이다. 화면에 한국어를 안 낸다」.
 * ⛔ Numbers come from src/data/workday-denominator.json, never typed by hand.
 * 🔴 Astro frontmatter (between the `---`) is TypeScript. JSX breaks the build.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../../consts';
import facts from '../../data/workday-denominator.json';
import BuyBar from '../../components/BuyBar.astro';

const f = facts as any;
const N = (n: number | null | undefined) =>
  n == null ? '—' : Number(n).toLocaleString('en-GB');
const pct = (x: number | null | undefined, d = 1) =>
  x == null ? '—' : `${(Number(x) * 100).toFixed(d)}%`;
const 일 = (n: number | null | undefined) =>
  n == null ? '—' : `${Number(n).toFixed(1)} days`;
const 달 = (s: string | null | undefined) =>
  !s ? '—' : `${String(s).slice(0, 4)}-${String(s).slice(4, 6)}`;
const 달이름 = (mm: string) => ([
  '', 'January', 'February', 'March', 'April', 'May', 'June',
  'July', 'August', 'September', 'October', 'November', 'December',
][Number(mm)] ?? mm);

const 달들 = Array.isArray(f.달마다) ? f.달마다 : [];
const 해줄 = Array.isArray(f.해줄) ? f.해줄 : [];
const 이음매 = Array.isArray(f.이음매) ? f.이음매 : [];
/* 달력 차례로 다시 세운다 — 자료는 평균 차례로 왔다 */
const 달력차례 = [...달들].sort((a: any, b: any) => String(a.달).localeCompare(String(b.달)));
const 모자란해 = 해줄.filter((r: any) => !r.온전한가);

const title = 'Korea’s monthly numbers have a moving denominator';
const description =
  `Korean workplaces operated ${일(f.적은달?.평균)} in an average February against `
  + `${일(f.많은달?.평균)} in July, and the annual figure fell ${pct(f.해사이몫)} between `
  + `${f.첫해?.해} and ${f.끝해?.해}. The series also breaks twice.`;
---

<Base title={title} description={description} canonical={`${SITE_URL}/data/workday-denominator`}>
  <article class="wrap">
    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      Every monthly figure out of Korea — output, shipments, hours, payroll — is a count of
      what happened across however many days the workplace was actually open. That count is
      itself published, and it moves. Across {N(f.달수)} months from {달(f.첫달)} to
      {' '}{달(f.끝달)}, an average February ran <b>{일(f.적은달?.평균)}</b> against
      {' '}<b>{일(f.많은달?.평균)}</b> in July — a {pct(f.달사이몫)} gap before anything
      economic has happened at all.
    </p>

    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>{pct(f.달사이몫)}</b><span>February vs July, long-run average</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>{pct(f.해사이몫)}</b><span>{f.첫해?.해} to {f.끝해?.해}, annual mean</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>{일(f.가장적은달?.값)}</b><span>thinnest month ({달(f.가장적은달?.달)})</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>{이음매.length}</b><span>breaks in the series</span></div>
    </div>

    <h2>The seasonal shape</h2>
    <p>
      Averaged over every year we hold, the calendar alone moves the denominator by
      {' '}{pct(f.달사이몫)}. A month-on-month comparison that ignores it is reading the
      calendar and calling it the economy.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Month</th>
            <th class="num">Mean operating days</th>
            <th class="num">Years counted</th>
            <th>Relative</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {달력차례.map((r: any) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{달이름(r.달)}</td>
              <td class="num">{일(r.평균)}</td>
              <td class="num">{N(r.해수)}</td>
              <td>
                <span
                  class="bar"
                  style={`width:${(((r.평균 ?? 0) - 16) / 6 * 100).toFixed(1)}%`}
                ></span>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Bars are scaled from 16 to 22 days so the differences are visible; they do not start
      at zero, and they are not meant to be read as magnitudes.
    </p>

    <h2>The long drift</h2>
    <p>
      The annual mean fell from {일(f.첫해?.평균)} in {f.첫해?.해} to
      {' '}{일(f.끝해?.평균)} in {f.끝해?.해} — <b>{pct(f.해사이몫)}</b> over the span.
      Any Korean monthly series compared across those years is being divided by a number
      that moved by roughly a tenth. <b>We do not say why it moved.</b> Working-hour law,
      substitute public holidays and survey redesign are all plausible; none of them is
      something we measured, so none of them appears here as a cause.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Year</th>
            <th class="num">Mean operating days</th>
            <th class="num">Months</th>
            <th>Complete</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {해줄.map((r: any) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{r.해}</td>
              <td class="num">{일(r.평균)}</td>
              <td class="num">{N(r.개월수)}</td>
              <td class="dim">{r.온전한가 ? 'Yes' : 'Partial year'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    {모자란해.length > 0 && (
      <p class="note">
        {N(모자란해.length)} of these years hold fewer than twelve months. They are marked
        rather than averaged in as though complete — a partial year is a different object
        from a full one, and putting them in one column without saying so is how a table
        starts lying.
      </p>
    )}

    <h2 class="warn-h">The series breaks — and the break has two sizes</h2>
    <p>
      The statistical office renumbers this survey periodically, and at each changeover the
      published figure steps. The source states a change rate at the joint. That rate
      compares December with January — <b>two months that are never alike</b>, because
      January is structurally one of the thinnest months of the year. So the stated step
      carries the season inside it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Below, each break is measured twice: once as the source states it, and once as the
      mean of the twelve months either side, which removes the season.
      <b> Both are true.</b> They answer different questions, and publishing only the
      larger one would make the break look worse than it is — publishing only the smaller
      one would hide what a reader of the raw file actually sees.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Break</th>
            <th class="num">As stated (month on month)</th>
            <th class="num">Twelve-month means</th>
            <th class="num">Months either side</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {이음매.map((j: any) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{달(j.전)} &rarr; {달(j.후)}</td>
              <td class="num">{j.못쟀다 ? '—' : pct(j.한달몫)}</td>
              <td class="num">{j.못쟀다 ? '—' : pct(j.열두달몫)}</td>
              <td class="num dim">
                {j.못쟀다 ? j.못쟀다 : `${N(j.앞달수)} / ${N(j.뒤달수)}`}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Where fewer than twelve months have accumulated after a break, we average what exists
      and print the count rather than waiting or padding. The most recent break has only a
      few months on the far side, and that figure will move as more arrive.
    </p>

    <h2 class="warn-h">What this page does not tell you</h2>
    <div class="warn">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>Why the count fell.</b> We measured the movement, not its cause. A page that
          counted days is not entitled to an explanation it did not test.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A spliced series.</b> We could publish one number running cleanly from
          {' '}{달(f.첫달)} to {달(f.끝달)} by applying a correction factor at each break.
          We do not, because that number would be ours rather than the statistical
          office's, and a reader could not check it against the source.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>How to adjust your own series.</b> Dividing by operating days is one choice
          among several, and the right one depends on what you are measuring. We draw the
          terrain; the needle is yours to set.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Anything about one firm or one sector.</b> This is a survey-wide figure. A
          single workplace's calendar is not in here.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p class="note">
      Source: {f._출처}. Collected {f._받은때}. Every figure on this page is computed at
      build time from <code>workday-denominator.json</code> by
      {' '}<code>scripts/measure-workday-denominator.mjs</code>; none is transcribed by
      hand. Months we could not read are dropped rather than filled with zero, and the
      counts above say how many went into each average.
    </p>

    <BuyBar />
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .wrap { max-width: 56rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 1.5rem 1rem 3rem; }
  h1 { font-size: 1.6rem; line-height: 1.25; margin: 0 0 .6rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.12rem; margin: 1.8rem 0 .5rem; }
  h2.warn-h { color: var(--warn, #9a3412); }
  .lead { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 1.2rem; }
  p { line-height: 1.65; }
  .stats {
    display: grid; gap: .6rem; margin: 1rem 0 1.4rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(9rem, 1fr));
  }
  .stat { padding: .7rem .8rem; border: 1px solid var(--line, #e5e5e5); border-radius: 6px; }
  .stat b { display: block; font-size: 1.25rem; line-height: 1.2; }
  .stat span { font-size: .78rem; color: var(--muted, #666); }
  .warn {
    padding: .8rem .95rem; border-left: 4px solid var(--accent, #0f4c81);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent, #0f4c81) 6%, transparent);
  }
  .note { font-size: .85rem; color: var(--muted, #555); }
  .scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: .85rem; margin: .6rem 0; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: .45rem .55rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line, #eee); }
  th { font-weight: 650; white-space: nowrap; }
  td.num, th.num { text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  td.dim, span.dim { color: var(--muted, #666); white-space: nowrap; }
  .bar {
    display: inline-block; height: .55rem; min-width: 1px; border-radius: 2px;
    background: var(--accent, #0f4c81); opacity: .55; vertical-align: middle;
  }
  ul { line-height: 1.65; padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  ul li { margin-bottom: .45rem; }
  code { font-size: .9em; }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    .stat { border-color: #333; }
    h2.warn-h { color: #fdba74; }
    td.dim, span.dim, .note, .stat span { color: #aaa; }
  }
</style>
